    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the response from critics is usually just that it's too expensive or that people will stop working, which feels like a pretty weak argument against the scale of what we're seeing. what are the actual, more serious economic objections to UBI specifically in the context of an AI-driven economy?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The core economic objections center on the risk of \"inflation of the man-hour\" if AI production becomes so efficient that human labor is undervalued and therefore underconsumed. Critics argue that in an AI-driven economy, the marginal product of labor could collapse to zero rather than just decline, potentially freeing up billions of people to pursue unproductive leisure or unrelated side hustles, which would have already been considered under the \"stop working\" criticism. Furthermore, some economists suggest that without a robust endogenous growth policy to shift value from human time to non-labor productivity, simply injecting cash into an already pre-mature AI ecosystem might not generate the necessary demand stimulus to sustain the new, less labor-intensive equilibrium.",
